Below is a brief explanation of the reasoning:

1. We first sorted the fields by their threat level (highest first). That order was:
  • Field_3 (threat 0.20)
  • Field_4 (threat 0.12)
  • Field_1 (threat 0.07)
  • Field_2 (threat 0.05)

2. The most threatened field (Field_3) needs 2 drones for full protection. One drone (Drone_4) is already on its way (moving_to_field) so we need one additional drone. We then sorted all the “remaining” drones by their distance to Field_3. Computing the minimal distance from each drone location to Field_3’s rectangular bounds revealed that Drone_3 (distance ≈8) was the closest. We therefore assign Drone_3 to help protect Field_3.

3. Next we distributed the rest of the drones among the remaining fields. Since our goal is to minimize overall damage, we favor fields with higher threat levels. Field_4, the next in the sorted order, requires 3 drones for full protection. Two drones (Drone_5 and Drone_6) are already protecting it; among the remaining drones Drone_1 was found to be the closest by a computed distance, so we reassign Drone_1 to help protect Field_4.

4. Finally, with the remaining drones (Drone_2, Drone_7, and Drone_8), we assign them to Field_1 (threat level 0.07) even though it still remains under‐protected (only 3 of its 4 required drones). Field_2, having the lowest threat level (0.05) and being less critical, is left without drone protection.

Thus the final assignments are as given above.
